2026-08-26 10:00:00 [info     ] model response: # Expectations
The paper describes using three sparse MuJoCo games introduced by Mazoure et al. (2019), one of which is Sparse Hopper. For the Sparse Hopper, the reward should only reflect the x‑position (forward progress) when the absolute x‑position exceeds a threshold (0.6). I therefore expect the submission to contain an implementation that modifies the standard Hopper environment (e.g., via a gym `Wrapper` or a custom `Env`) to produce this sparse reward, so that it can be instantiated and used for the experiments on sparse tasks.

# Reality
The provided files include:
- `Refine_mujoco/environment.py` – a `RetrainEnv` wrapper for resetting to critical states, but it only operates on standard gym environments.
- `Refine_mujoco/retrain.py` – a training script that accepts an `--env` argument, default `Hopper-v3`, and instantiates the (dense) environment using `gym.make(env_name)`.
- `Refine_mujoco/retrain.sh` – an example script that explicitly passes `--env "Hopper-v3"`.
- `Refine_mujoco/baseline/train.py` – trains a PPO agent on standard `Hopper-v3`.
- `Refine_mujoco/masknet/train.py` and related files – train the mask network on `Hopper-v3`.
- `Refine_mujoco/masknet/fid_test.py` – fidelity testing uses `gym.make("HalfCheetah-v3")` or `Hopper-v3`.

None of these files define a sparse Hopper variant by wrapping or subclassing the reward function. There is no custom gym environment registration, no reward‑modification wrapper, and no code that sets up a sparse Hopper environment as described in the paper. The submission only contains dense MuJoCo environments; the sparse version is completely absent.

# Score
Score: 0. The criterion requires an actual implementation of the Sparse Hopper environment that can be initialized and used in experiments. The submitted files contain no such implementation, so the resolution is not met.
